{
 "name": "clique",
 "category": "clique",
 "source": "The second DIMACS implementation challenge (1992-93; M. A. Trick, D. S. Johnson), its archive https://archive.dimacs.rutgers.edu/pub/challenge/graph/ (the address http://archive.dimacs.rutgers.edu/pub/challenge/graph/benchmarks/clique/ redirects there). Graphs, in the DIMACS binary format: the files benchmarks/clique/*.clq.b with at most 1000 vertices by the table contents.tex (58 of 66), and the graphs of benchmarks/volume/Clique that are not in benchmarks/clique and have at most 1000 vertices (gen200_p0.9_44, gen200_p0.9_55, gen400_p0.9_55, gen400_p0.9_65, gen400_p0.9_75, C125.9, C250.9, C500.9, C1000.9, DSJC500.5, DSJC1000.5; the volume's 23 graphs that are also in benchmarks/clique (16 of them within the size rule) are byte-identical copies of those files, checked, and are not fetched; C2000.5, C2000.9 and C4000.5 are above the size rule). Read for the reference: benchmarks/clique/contents.tex (the table of the clique benchmarks), solvers/results.dmclique and solvers/results.dfmax (the steering committee's results files), the solution files benchmarks/clique/SOLUTIONS/*.sol of the graphs fetched (25 of 27: san2.sol names no graph of the directory and has no s line, hamming10-2.sol belongs to a graph above the size rule), and the comment lines of the graph files. Pinned as records, not parsed: translators/binformat/README.binformat (the binary format), doc/ccformat.tex (the text format and the solution lines), gen_info.tex (the problem statement), benchmarks/clique/LAST_UPDATESep23 (the correction of the p_hat files), solvers/README (what dfmax and dmclique are), benchmarks/volume/instruct.tex (the volume's test set and its families) and benchmarks/volume/Clique/Appendix.tex (the volume's results form, empty)",
 "terms": "the archive states no licence and no terms of use for these files. The challenge's general information (gen_info.tex) says: 'With author's permission, submitted inputs and generators will be made available to Challenge participants; some may be included in the set of DIMACS benchmarks.' Fetched by hash, not redistributed here.",
 "reference": "the maximum clique size the source states for each graph (kernel only). Read: '[OPT = k]' in solvers/results.dmclique ('We also list the true maximum clique size, when known'; '[OPT = ??]' where not known); the column 'Clique Size' of benchmarks/clique/contents.tex (a plain number; after '$\\ge$' a lower bound); the column 'Max' of solvers/results.dfmax (the exact branch and bound code dfmax, on the graphs it finished); the line 's cqu k' of a solution file whose comment says 'Solution is optimal.' and whose k listed vertices (numbered from 0) are a clique of the graph; the comment lines 'k max clique' (san, gen) and 'Largest Clique: k' (keller) of the graph files. Every such statement found for a graph must give the same value, and no lower bound ('$\\ge$ k' of the table, 'Clique Size: k' of the brock and MANN files, which list the clique their generator built) may exceed it; this holds for all 56 graphs used. 50 have '[OPT = k]'; c-fat500-10 has only its solution file (126); the five gen graphs have only their comment line. The source also publishes answers: the 25 solution files and the clique lists in the comments of the 12 brock and 2 MANN files, each a clique of exactly the reference size. The values were not re-proved here.",
 "files": [
